WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:10.170
One way to improve the accuracy of pathway
analysis is to use the best of both of these

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.700
methods.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:16.270
We can use an enrichment approach to score
the pathways and then we also score the pathways

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:21.870
using a topology-based approach that identifies
how perturbed that particular pathway is.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:25.900
The benefit of this is that you are less likely
to have false positives because you're now

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:30.330
using two different types of evidence to score
each pathway.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:34.660
Under this model, the enrichment approach
first assesses the abundance of significant

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:36.960
genes in the dataset.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.220
For example, if we have 20,000 genes in our
dataset.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:46.149
After we threshold for significant genes either
by fold change or by p-value, we are left

00:00:46.149 --> 00:00:51.960
with approximately 10% of the genes as being
significantly differentially expressed.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.510
Based on this overall abundance of significant
genes, when we look at any pathway, we would

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:01.540
expect to see 10% of the genes in that pathway
to also be significant just by random chance

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.090
alone.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:07.540
But if we see more than 10% of significant
genes in a pathway, then we consider that

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.729
pathway is enriched.

00:01:09.729 --> 00:01:14.909
It has a greater number of significant genes
than we would have expected by chance alone.

00:01:14.909 --> 00:01:18.240
This is how the enrichment model approach
works.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.729
You'll notice that the pathway diagram does
not have any interactions because this method

00:01:22.729 --> 00:01:26.909
does not consider the interactions among genes
in the pathway.

00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:31.460
Any kind of enrichment analysis approach,
a pathway is just a bag of genes.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:36.680
Now let's talk about how we calculate how
perturb that pathway is.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:40.859
In this particular model, we take the fold
change for a given gene that was observed

00:01:40.859 --> 00:01:45.310
in our data and then based on its position
and its role in the pathway, we take that

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:49.820
fold change that was observed and we propagate
it to the next gene that's directly downstream

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:51.229
of it.

00:01:51.229 --> 00:01:56.340
And then we propagate it again to the next
gene until we reach the end of the pathway.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.390
These interactions could be activation or
could be inhibition and the model take those

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:04.849
into consideration and do the signal changes
that are appropriate.

00:02:04.849 --> 00:02:10.000
The model even takes into consideration when
one gene signals multiple genes that are downstream

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.700
or if we have several genes that concentrate
their signal onto a single gene.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.480
Each one of those situations is taken into
consideration.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.060
The model does that for each and every single
significant gene on every single pathway in

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:24.810
our dataset.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:30.050
Then the model sum all of the gene level perturbations
to assess how perturb the overall pathway

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:31.280
is.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.860
This is a much closer approximation to how
we would assess the pathway from a biological

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:39.640
perspective rather than just counting the
number of significant genes.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.660
Let’s look at how this works on a real pathway.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:48.090
In this example, we have a single significant
gene in this particular pathway.

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:52.350
Here, we're looking at the insulin signaling
pathway.

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:56.870
The way the enrichment model is going to assess
this pathway is it's going to count the number

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:59.070
of significant genes.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:03.400
In this case, we have one significant gene
and it's going to compare that to the total

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:06.320
number of about 50 genes in the pathway.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:12.400
So, our ratio of significant versus non-significant
genes is 1 out of 50.

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:16.430
This the major limitation of the enrichment
model where the model may conclude that 1

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:20.460
out of 50 genes is not significantly enriched.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:25.270
It also does not recognize whether the significant
gene that we input in is located at the very

00:03:25.270 --> 00:03:29.120
up stream or down stream of the entire pathway.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:33.920
Both of those situations have the exact same
ratio of significant genes to non-significant

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:35.770
genes in this pathway.

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:40.000
Therefore, the enrichment model would not
be able to tell the difference between these

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.170
two situations.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:46.370
But as biologists, we can tell that this in
the situation where the significant gene is

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:50.540
at the end of the pathway is very different
then if the gene is at the beginning of the

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:51.930
pathway.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:55.240
Let's look at the same example from a perturbation
perspective.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:59.709
Here, instead of just counting the number
of genes, we're also going to look at the

00:03:59.709 --> 00:04:01.430
interactions.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:05.750
If we zoom in on this particular significant
gene we see that it's going to have a direct

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:10.180
impact on the upper cascade at the top of
the pathway, the genes in the central part

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:14.670
of the pathway, and also the genes in the
lower part of the pathway.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:19.739
If we take this fold change, in this case,
we have a 5 fold change in expression and

00:04:19.739 --> 00:04:24.190
we propagate it to all of the downstream genes
until we can't go any further, we see that

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:28.440
this one specific gene is going to affect
all of the other genes in this particular

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:29.840
system.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.150
This makes sense because this is the main
entry point to this particular system.

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:39.050
Therefore, when we sum up all of the gene
level perturbations, we would be able to identify

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:43.850
that this pathway is highly perturbed given
this one significant gene because it is crucial

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:47.090
in the regulation of this particular system.

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:50.789
We would not be able to identify that using
an enrichment model.

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:56.169
Now, let's contrast this example with a slightly
different example.

00:04:56.169 --> 00:04:59.389
Here we're seeing the adherence junction pathway.

00:04:59.389 --> 00:05:03.729
We have the same insulin signaling receptor
in this particular system.

00:05:03.729 --> 00:05:08.150
If we were to assess this from an enrichment
model, again we have one significant gene

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:12.059
out of roughly 50 genes contained on this
pathway.

00:05:12.059 --> 00:05:16.289
We will see the exact same ratio as in our
previous example.

00:05:16.289 --> 00:05:20.280
The enrichment model would not be able to
tell the difference whether this pathway is

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.749
more important to us or the previous pathway.

00:05:23.749 --> 00:05:27.819
The model would score relatively the same
because it has the same number of significant

00:05:27.819 --> 00:05:31.830
genes versus the total number of genes on
the pathway.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:35.979
But when we look at the interactions among
genes in this particular system, we see that

00:05:35.979 --> 00:05:40.550
in this case, the insulin signaling receptor
does not propagate its signal to any other

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:43.330
genes in this particular system.

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:47.480
In this system, the receptor plays a much
less crucial role.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:52.030
If we try to assess how perturb this pathway
is, we're going to see that this pathway is

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:57.290
much less perturbed and therefore much less
significant compared to our previous example.

00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:01.800
This is the key difference between analysis
of pathways from an enrichment model versus

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:04.089
a perturbation model.

00:06:04.089 --> 00:06:09.089
One way to overcome these discrepancies is
to use both forms of evidence.

00:06:09.089 --> 00:06:13.990
In this two-way plot, we'll see on the horizontal
axis we have the minus log of the p value

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:17.400
that came from the enrichment analysis approach.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:21.210
What that means is those pathways with a lot
of evidence for being enriched are over the

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:23.879
right-hand side of this graph.

00:06:23.879 --> 00:06:27.540
We also score the pathways using the perturbation
model.

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:32.379
The minus log of the p value that came from
that perturbation analysis is plotted on the

00:06:32.379 --> 00:06:34.229
vertical axis.

00:06:34.229 --> 00:06:38.710
So those pathways with a lot of evidence being
perturbed will be along the top edge of this

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:39.759
graph.

00:06:39.759 --> 00:06:44.669
Ideally, we want to find those pathways that
have both types of evidence.

00:06:44.669 --> 00:06:48.419
They will be located in the brown section
of the graph and those pathways are least

00:06:48.419 --> 00:06:52.650
likely to be false positives because they
have confirming evidence from both types of

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:54.320
approaches.

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:58.900
For example, here is an actual plot of a data
set that we analyzed.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:03.499
We will see that we have a pathway over here
that has a lot of evidence for being enriched.

00:07:03.499 --> 00:07:08.300
Again, all that means is it has a large number
of significant genes that are contained on

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:10.749
this particular pathway.

00:07:10.749 --> 00:07:16.050
Here we have a pathway that has a lot of evidence
for being perturbed but it's not very enriched.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:20.759
Again, what that means is that there are probably
some key genes that are responsible for the

00:07:20.759 --> 00:07:23.419
regulation of this particular pathway.

00:07:23.419 --> 00:07:28.400
Now, here is our number one pathway in this
particular example.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:32.180
This particular pathway has both types of
evidence.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:37.840
It is the most perturbed pathway and it also
happens to be one of the most enriched pathways.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:42.220
So this is probably the first pathway that
we want to look at when assessing pathways.

